"ở hình 1 khi chưa appect bên nào thì 2 bên sẽ hiện diff ở từng từ là 1: flex, flex-col 2: 6xl, lg... nhưng khi accept bên trái vào center rồi mất luôn hight của nhưng từ difff"

Translation: "In image 1, when no side accepted yet, both sides show word-level diff: 1: flex, flex-col; 2: 6xl, lg... but when I accept the left side into center, [it] loses the highlight of those diff words."

"có cách nào tối ưu chỗ này khi tôi accept 1 bên rồi thì vẫn giữ highlight của từng từ để tôi biết điểm thay đổi để merge kết hơp cả 2"

"is there a way to optimize this spot so that when I accept one side, [it] still keeps the word highlight so I know the change point to merge/combine both."

So the highlights they refer to are the ones shown in the two side panes ("2 bên sẽ hiện diff ở từng từ"). After accepting, those side-pane word highlights disappear. They want them to remain.

The key insight is: the word highlights in the SIDE panes disappear because of `chunk.state !== 'initial'` check. My fix addresses exactly this.

But should I also add word highlighting to the center pane? The center pane currently only shows the overall chunk fill. After accepting the left side, the center contains the left text. To "merge combine both", the user edits the center manually. Showing word-level diff in the center would require comparing center vs base (or center vs the unaccepted side). That's a more complex change and possibly what the user ultimately wants, but their explicit complaint is about the side-pane highlights disappearing.

Let me focus on the minimal, correct fix: keep the side-pane word highlights visible after resolution (dimmed), so the user can still see exactly which words differ between the two sides and combine them.

However, I should consider: is there a risk this is considered "wrong" by the product design (JetBrains behavior)? The comment says "Emphasis only helps while the chunk is still a live question." But the user explicitly wants it kept. Since this is their tool and they're asking for the optimization, I'll implement it as a feature.

Now, one more consideration: should I make this configurable? The user asks "có cách nào tối ưu chỗ này" (is there a way to optimize this spot). They want a fix/feature. I think the cleanest is to just keep the highlights dimmed after resolution. But I could also add a toolbar toggle. Given the existing "Highlight" toolbar has modes (words vs lines), maybe there's already a highlight mode toggle. Let me check toolbar.ts for HighlightMode.

